WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:04.500
we take a look at
Bigtable's monitoring UI

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.840
to find out our
performance edge cases.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.640
Will we scale to
meet the challenge?

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.200
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.474
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.474 --> 00:00:13.890
When you're dealing
with something

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.410
at the scale of
Bigtable, performance

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.150
is always on your mind.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.640
Bigtable's ability to
handle billion user systems

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.520
happens almost entirely behind
the scenes, where front-ends

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.010
can coordinate balance
and optimize your access

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.470
patterns without you
having to lift a finger.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.650
But even the best systems need
a little attention now and then.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.160
Thankfully, the monitoring
UI is here to help.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.466
The Bigtable UI in
the Cloud console

00:00:37.466 --> 00:00:39.840
has a built-in monitoring tool
that automatically reports

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.930
how your nodes, clusters, and
instances are performing--

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.510
showing things like
CPU utilization, error

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:49.531
rate, and storage utilization
all in a handy graph form,

00:00:49.531 --> 00:00:51.780
so that at a glance, you can
see how your systems have

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:52.800
been performing.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.570
And while having this
graph data is great,

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.850
what's really important
is finding the situations

00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:58.854
where it actually
tells you something.

00:00:58.854 --> 00:01:00.270
Here's a few common
use cases that

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.040
are worth keeping an eye on.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.810
As mentioned before,
Bigtable works

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.030
by keeping a set of
front-end nodes, which

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.040
are responsible for routing
and bouncing traffic

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.750
to the back-end tables.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:11.700
When you create your
Bigtable instance,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:13.620
you specify the number
of nodes you want.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:15.330
And using the
Monitoring tool, you

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.390
can quickly see if it's
time to add more nodes.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:20.280
On our Instances tab,
we can take a look

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.740
at the CPU utilization
table, which

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.180
has a recommended max line.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.570
This line represents the best
practices for CPU utilization.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.290
And if you eclipse this line
for a number of minutes,

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.722
it's generally a good idea to
add more nodes to your cluster.

00:01:34.722 --> 00:01:36.930
Now, another common performance
problem with Bigtable

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.850
is asymmetric reads and
writes on a cluster.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.180
For example, suppose
that 25% of your reads

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:45.210
are going to a small number
of tablets within a cluster,

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.049
while the rest are spread
evenly across the other tablets.

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:49.590
When Bigtable detects
this happening,

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.840
it will redistribute
the existing tablets,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.120
so that reads are spread
as evenly as possible

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.800
across the entire cluster.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.050
Now, we can detect this
pattern in the monitoring tool

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.770
by keeping an eye on the CPU
utilization of the hottest

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:02.720
node graph.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.170
That's a lot of air quotes.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.850
If the hottest
node is frequently

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.800
above the recommended
value, even when

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.379
your average CPU
utilization is reasonable,

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:12.420
then you'll need to go
back and check your schema

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.310
design to update your
access patterns accordingly.

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:18.240
Now, if you want to drill
deeper into your schema design

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.930
challenges, then you might
want to take a look at the Key

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.810
Visualizer tool, which provides
a really fancy heat map

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.610
visualizer that can
help you figure out

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.050
where your schema design
might be going off the rails.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.014
But that's a topic
for another episode.

00:02:31.014 --> 00:02:32.430
If you're interested
to know more,

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.500
then check out the article
linked in the description

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.000
below.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.470
And don't forget to subscribe
to our YouTube Cloud

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.289
channel for other great
Performance Atlas content--

00:02:40.289 --> 00:02:41.830
because when it
comes to performance,

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.320
every millisecond counts.

